j['pupilPages']=[page('Shared sources | Warm-up and checkpoint','All numbers are constructed classroom records. Warm-up letters M/N are separate from checkpoint letters A/B and U/V.',[txt('Warm-up only: M has 60 beats/min and 50 mL/beat; N has 80 beats/min and 50 mL/beat. W1 calculate N. W2 test “all hormones affect every cell equally”. W3 classify a pulmonary artery using direction.'),tb(['Checkpoint pump','Heart rate (beats/min)','Stroke volume (mL/beat)'],pump_rows),txt('Pump output = heart rate × stroke volume. 1,000 mL = 1 L. The table does not measure health or fitness.'),tb(['Exchange case','Relative area','Relative gradient','Relative thickness'],diff_rows),txt('Idealised relative index = area × gradient ÷ thickness, with the same omitted constant. These are model inputs, not measured human gas-exchange data.'),txt('Q1 context: glucose absorption has raised blood glucose. Q2 starts with oxygenated blood leaving the lungs. Q4 concerns quiet inhalation and oxygen exchange into arriving blood.')]),page('Supported checkpoint | 20 core marks','Same concepts and marks. Optional word bank: pancreas; liver; insulin; glycogen; atrium; ventricle; aorta. Record support used.',[response(q1+' Use four short points.',3),response(q2,3),response(q3,3),response(q4+' Link each feature to its effect.',3),response(q5,3)]),page('Standard checkpoint | 20 core marks','Complete Q1–Q5 using the shared sources. Show working and units.',[response(q1,3),response(q2,3),response(q3,3),response(q4,3),response(q5,3)]),page('Challenge checkpoint | Same 20 core marks','Complete the same core first. The final audit is optional and unscored.',[response(q1,3),response(q2,3),response(q3,3),response(q4,3),response(q5,3),txt('Optional audit: explain what additional evidence you would need before using pump records to judge whether oxygen supply meets a tissue’s demand. Use spare paper or an oral response; no extra marks.')]),page('Feedback and next step | Keep the first attempt','Complete after the independent checkpoint. This is a selected-topic check, not a GCSE grade.',[response('Record: route used; calculator; read-aloud; any content prompts or additional support.',2),tb(['Item','Focus','Mark /4','One next step'],[['Q1','Insulin response','',''],['Q2','Route and vessel definition','',''],['Q3','Pump calculation and units','',''],['Q4','Ventilation and exchange','',''],['Q5','Model comparison and limit','','']]),response('Total /20: ____ . L1. Preserve your first answer; write one correction and explain the scientific reason.',4),response('E1. One idea I can explain now. E2. One precise practice for my next attempt.',3)])]
j['answers']=[qa('Retrieval, We do and hinge','R1 blood. R2 ventilation moves air; diffusion is net particle movement down a concentration gradient. W1 M = 3,000 mL/min = 3 L/min; N = 4,000 mL/min = 4 L/min. W2 target-cell recognition/responsiveness matters. W3 pulmonary artery is an artery because it leaves the heart. Output alone does not establish health. Correct hinge: record support, attempt first and review afterwards.'),qa('Q1: 4 marks, same across all routes','Award one mark each: pancreas [1]; insulin [1]; one valid response such as increased glucose uptake in responsive tissues or increased glycogen storage in liver/muscle [1]; blood glucose decreases towards its usual range [1]. Do not require beta-cell terminology. Do not credit insulin becoming glycogen. Some glucose uptake is insulin-independent.'),qa('Q2: 4 marks, same across all routes','Lungs → left atrium [1] → left ventricle [1] → aorta [1] → body. Artery carries blood away from the heart [1]. Accept LA/LV if meaning is clear. Oxygen-rich is not a sufficient definition; pulmonary artery is the counterexample.'),qa('Q3: 4 marks, same across all routes','A: 75 × 80 [1] = 6,000 mL/min [1]; 6 L/min [1]; output also depends on stroke volume, so heart rate alone is insufficient [1]. Accept consistent error-carried-forward for the conversion method if a previous arithmetic slip occurred. If B is used as evidence: 100 × 50 = 5,000 mL/min = 5 L/min, despite its higher heart rate. A correct unsupported final 6 L/min can receive the result and conversion marks, but not the explicit-working mark.'),qa('Q4: 4 marks, same across all routes','Thoracic volume increases [1]; pressure falls below atmospheric/surrounding air pressure [1]; oxygen diffuses from alveolar air into arriving blood down its concentration gradient [1]; a thin barrier shortens diffusion distance [1]. For the third mark require direction and gradient. Cellular respiration and ventilation alone do not name the exchange mechanism.'),qa('Q5: 4 marks, same across all routes','Thickness changes, from 1 to 2 [1]. V = 4 × 3 / 2 = 6 [1]. U = 12, so V has half U’s index [1]. One specific limit [1]: relative uncalibrated inputs, omitted proportionality/diffusion coefficient, uniform fixed barrier/gradient, no changing blood flow or ventilation, or no tissue-demand information. Do not accept only “it is a model” without explaining the limitation. Allow consistent comparison reasoning after an arithmetic slip; do not award two marks for one unexplained value.'),qa('Optional unscored audit','A defensible answer identifies oxygen content, distribution to the relevant tissue, exchange effectiveness and/or tissue oxygen demand as additional evidence. Explain why a volume per minute alone cannot show oxygen adequacy. Do not use this answer to add marks to the 20-mark core.'),qa('Marking and feedback','Five items × four marks = 20. Record route and support separately from marks; scores from different support conditions are not directly interchangeable. This small checkpoint has no official grade boundaries. Use item evidence: Q1 signal/response reteach; Q2 trace left-heart route; Q3 units and multiplication; Q4 volume/pressure and gradient; Q5 controlled factors and model scope.'),qa('L1 and exit','Accept a specific correction tied to an item and a scientific reason. E1 names a demonstrable idea; E2 identifies a concrete practice, such as a fresh mL-to-L calculation or annotating a pressure sequence. Generic confidence alone is not the intended evidence.')]
j['teacherNotes'] += ['Assessment sequence: analogous recap and joint examples, then 18 uninterrupted minutes for the selected 20-mark route. Teacher feedback begins only after the first attempt is paused or collected.','The interactive panel uses only warm-up cases M/N and general route principles. It does not reveal checkpoint A/B output, U/V calculations or the Q1–Q5 mark scheme.','Give focused feedback to one chosen item during the three-minute review and complete any remaining marking afterwards. Preserve original responses and corrections as separate evidence.','Same core concepts and marks across routes. The supported word bank and reading support affect interpretation, so record them; do not assign a fixed attainment identity from a route or score.']
j['interactive']=interactive('Warm-up prediction desk','Original recap only. Complete prediction and test before the independent checkpoint.',[step('Why can a hormone carried in blood produce different responses in different cells?','Cells differ in whether they recognise and respond to the signal.','Blood delivers every hormone only to a single cell.',v1,'Circulation distributes hormones; target-cell recognition and response matter.','Blood distribution is not a one-cell delivery route.'),step('Why is the pulmonary artery an artery?','It carries blood away from the heart.','It must carry oxygen-rich blood.',v2,'Direction defines an artery, including the heart-to-lung route.','The pulmonary artery is relatively oxygen-poor; oxygen content does not define the vessel class.'),step('What is warm-up N’s model output: 80 beats/min × 50 mL/beat?','4,000 mL/min, or 4 L/min.','400 mL/min, or 0.4 L/min.',v4,'80 × 50 = 4,000. Divide mL by 1,000 to obtain litres.','The multiplication is ten times too small. Keep both factors and check the conversion.')])
